<%#
  Flash strip. A message bar gets a PLAIN glyph, never an icon tile: a soft -50 tile on a
  -50 surface is invisible, and a filled one shouts and adds height. Tint, hairline border
  and a leading glyph carry the semantics.

  role="status" for informational tones, role="alert" for warning/danger, so a screen
  reader interrupts only when something actually went wrong.
%>
<%= turbo_frame_tag "flash" do %>
  <%#
    The strip is always in the DOM, even with nothing in it, because a message can be raised after
    the page has rendered -- `barcode_items/create.js.erb` appends one -- and JS needs somewhere to
    put it. `data-flash-region` is that hook, as stable a contract as `data-flash`.

    An always-present wrapper would otherwise cost 24px of `pt-6` on every page in the app, so the
    strip hides itself while it holds no message: `.flash-strip:not(:has([data-flash]))` in
    application.css. CSS rather than JS, so nothing has to remember to toggle it, and appending a
    message is all it takes to reveal it.
  %>
  <div class="flash-strip px-4 pt-6 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="space-y-3" data-flash-region>
      <% if flash.any? %>
        <%# Read through flash[key], not flash.each. The memflash gem stores a large message
            in the cache and leaves a lookup key in the cookie; only the reader resolves it,
            so iterating pairs renders the key itself. %>
        <%# Not every flash entry is a message. `trigger_csv_download` is a boolean flag that
            survives the redirect in `handle_csv_export` and tells the page to start a background
            download; rendering it here would draw a message bar reading "true", because `true` is
            not blank. Anything that is a signal rather than a sentence belongs in this list. %>
        <% flash.keys.each do |key| %>
          <% next if EssentialsUiHelper::NON_MESSAGE_FLASH_KEYS.include?(key.to_s) %>
          <% message = flash[key] %>
          <% next if message.blank? %>
          <%= render "shared/essentials/flash_message", key: key, message: message %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
<% end %>
